     <div class="aside">
       <p class="subhead">A note on scale</p>
       <p>A cell is mostly water, and it is tempting to reach for Navier–Stokes. But at a cell's size the Reynolds number is about 10⁻⁵. The inertial term that makes those equations hard vanishes, and what remains is Stokes flow: linear, time-reversible, no turbulence. At the cell, water matters as the solvent for the ion gradients that set the pacemaker's rate, and the right equations are Hodgkin–Huxley, not Navier–Stokes. Navier–Stokes is the physics of the infant's aorta, where the Reynolds number runs into the thousands and the flow is pulsatile. It describes each body from the inside. After birth no fluid connects the two bodies; what they share is heat, pressure, sound, light and air.</p>
